+// circle-hid-reenum.patch: the escalation from the endpoint-level recovery above to a forced re-enumeration of the
+// whole device.
+//
+// Measured on the GeeekPi 7" case's touch screen (0416:c169) after its own OSD menu changed the touch rotation, and
+// unchanged by a cold power cycle: 4201 endpoint resets and 4201 resubmits that all reported success, endpoint state
+// 2 (halted) for ever, xHCI completion code 36 (split transaction error), and not one report delivered. Pulling the
+// USB cable out and putting it back fixed it completely. So reset-from-halted is mechanically fine and still cannot
+// revive the endpoint, while a fresh address and device context can - and that is what a re-enumeration gives.
+//
+// ReEnumAfterGaveUpMs: how long the loop must have been in its given-up state (m_nErrorCount at
+// MaxConsecutiveErrors) before Poll () escalates. Errors come back as fast as the transfers complete, so the count
+// reaches 50 within milliseconds and the loop then retries once a second: 10 s means 50 immediate errors plus about
+// ten reset-and-resubmit cycles that each reported success and still produced no report. That is far past any
+// transient (a bus glitch or a device hiccup is over in one or two retries) and past the hub's 510 ms power-good and
+// 100 ms port-reset delays, so a device that is merely still coming up is never touched. It is short enough that the
+// screen is back before whoever is poking at it has given up.
+static const unsigned ReEnumAfterGaveUpMs = 10000;
+
+// MaxReEnums: a replug fixes this in one go, so three re-enumerations that each fail to produce a working endpoint
+// mean a broken device or cable, and more port resets will not help. The budget is then spent, the device is left to
+// the endpoint-level loop, and the diagnostics say REENUM-EXHAUSTED. The tries are at least ReEnumAfterGaveUpMs
+// apart by construction (each one has to earn its own MaxConsecutiveErrors run and its own silence), and a device
+// that is simply unplugged is removed by Circle's own plug-and-play and never polled again, so it cannot spend the
+// budget at all.
+static const unsigned MaxReEnums = 3;
+
+// ReEnumForgiveMs: the budget is handed back when the endpoint has been armed (state 1 with a request queued) and
+// error-free for this long, which is a real recovery and not a device that re-halts on its first transfer. An idle
+// touch screen sends no reports at all, so the health test cannot wait for one. 30 s is three times the escalation
+// delay, so a device that keeps dying cannot refund itself faster than it spends.
+static const unsigned ReEnumForgiveMs = 30000;
